From: nuno407 <11352538+nuno407@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:26:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/45] cpuidle: tegra: keep only WFI when the secure monitor
 can't resume CPUs

On the Surface RT the kernel runs in the non-secure world under a secure
monitor, registered as a Trusted Foundations variant that has no
set_cpu_boot_addr or prepare_idle. Entering C7 (core power-gating) or CC6
hangs the machine, which is why the EFI boot needs cpuidle.off=1. Disable
the power-gating states in that case, so the driver registers with C1 only
and the command line flag is no longer required.
---
 drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-tegra.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-tegra.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-tegra.c
index aca907a62..4942bd314 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-tegra.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-tegra.c
@@ -356,6 +356,21 @@ static int tegra_cpuidle_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (tegra_pmc_get_suspend_mode() < TEGRA_SUSPEND_LP2)
 		tegra_cpuidle_disable_state(TEGRA_CC6);
 
+	/*
+	 * A power-gated core restarts through the CPU reset vector.  When the
+	 * kernel runs in the non-secure world under a secure monitor that
+	 * can't be told where to resume (the Surface RT's Trusted Foundations
+	 * ops offer only L2 cache services), the core comes back
+	 * into the monitor and never returns to Linux: the machine hangs as
+	 * soon as C7 or CC6 is entered.  Keep only the WFI state then.
+	 */
+	if (trusted_foundations_registered() &&
+	    !firmware_ops->set_cpu_boot_addr) {
+		pr_info("secure firmware can't set the CPU resume address, power-gating states disabled\n");
+		tegra_cpuidle_disable_state(TEGRA_C7);
+		tegra_cpuidle_disable_state(TEGRA_CC6);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Required suspend-resume functionality, which is provided by the
 	 * Tegra-arch core and PMC driver, is unavailable if PM-sleep option
